Aim

To simulate the dominant TE₁₀ mode in a WR-90 rectangular waveguide using MATLAB and determine its cutoff frequency, propagation constant, and electric- and magnetic-field distributions at 10 GHz.

Procedure
Open MATLAB and create a new script for the TE₁₀ mode simulation.
Enter the WR-90 waveguide dimensions: width 22.86 mm, height 10.16 mm, and length 100 mm.
Set the operating frequency to 10 GHz and select the TE₁₀ mode.
Calculate the cutoff frequency and propagation constant of the selected mode.
Generate the electric-field and magnetic-field distributions over the waveguide cross-section.
Plot the normalized E-field and H-field distributions.
Compare the obtained cutoff frequency with the theoretical/reference value.
Calculate the percentage deviation between the simulated and theoretical cutoff frequencies.
Check whether the operating frequency is above the cutoff frequency.
Record the calculated values and field distributions for analysis.

Program
clc;
clear;
close all;

%% TE10 MODE SIMULATION - WR90 RECTANGULAR WAVEGUIDE

% Constants
c = 3e8; % Speed of light (m/s)

% Waveguide dimensions
a = 22.86e-3; % Width (m)
b = 10.16e-3; % Height (m)
L = 100e-3; % Length (m)

% Operating frequency
f = 10e9; % 10 GHz

% TE10 mode
m = 1;
n = 0;

%% Cutoff frequency

fc = (c/2)*sqrt((m/a)^2 + (n/b)^2);

%% Free-space wave number

k0 = 2pif/c;

%% Propagation constant

beta = k0*sqrt(1-(fc/f)^2);

%% Percentage deviation

% Theoretical cutoff frequency for TE10
fc_theory = c/(2*a);

% Simulated/analytical value
fc_sim = fc;

deviation = abs(fc_sim-fc_theory)/fc_theory*100;

%% Display results

fprintf('---------------------------------------------\n');
fprintf(' TE10 MODE - WR90 WAVEGUIDE\n');
fprintf('---------------------------------------------\n');

fprintf('Operating Frequency = %.2f GHz\n',f/1e9);
fprintf('Waveguide Width (a) = %.2f mm\n',a1000);
fprintf('Waveguide Height (b) = %.2f mm\n',b1000);
fprintf('Waveguide Length (L) = %.2f mm\n',L*1000);

fprintf('\nTheoretical Cutoff Frequency = %.4f GHz\n',fc_theory/1e9);
fprintf('Simulated Cutoff Frequency = %.4f GHz\n',fc_sim/1e9);
fprintf('Percentage Deviation = %.4f %%\n',deviation);

fprintf('\nPropagation Constant beta = %.4f rad/m\n',beta);

if f > fc
fprintf('Mode Status = TE10 PROPAGATES\n');
else
fprintf('Mode Status = TE10 BELOW CUTOFF\n');
end

%% Field grid

x = linspace(0,a,150);
y = linspace(0,b,100);

[X,Y] = meshgrid(x,y);

%% TE10 Electric field

% For TE10, dominant electric field is Ey
Ey = sin(pi*X/a);

% Normalize
Ey = Ey/max(abs(Ey(:)));

%% TE10 Magnetic field

% Hz distribution for TE10
Hz = cos(pi*X/a);

% Normalize
Hz = Hz/max(abs(Hz(:)));

%% E-field distribution

figure;

imagesc(x1000,y1000,Ey);
axis xy;
xlabel('Waveguide Width x (mm)');
ylabel('Waveguide Height y (mm)');
title('TE_{10} Mode - Normalized E-Field Distribution');
colorbar;

%% H-field distribution

figure;

imagesc(x1000,y1000,Hz);
axis xy;
xlabel('Waveguide Width x (mm)');
ylabel('Waveguide Height y (mm)');
title('TE_{10} Mode - Normalized H-Field Distribution');
colorbar;

%% E-field 3D visualization

figure;

surf(X1000,Y1000,Ey);
shading interp;
xlabel('x (mm)');
ylabel('y (mm)');
zlabel('Normalized E-field');
title('TE_{10} Mode - E-Field');
colorbar;

%% H-field 3D visualization

figure;

surf(X1000,Y1000,Hz);
shading interp;
xlabel('x (mm)');
ylabel('y (mm)');
zlabel('Normalized H-field');
title('TE_{10} Mode - H-Field');
colorbar;

